\section{Make the Output a Total Function of the Inputs}

In chapter~6 of \emph{Introduction to Computer Organization}, Robert G. Plantz distinguishes combinational circuits, whose outputs depend on present inputs, from sequential circuits, whose outputs can depend on previous inputs. A procedural HDL block can describe either one. The syntax of a conditional is not enough to tell a synthesis tool which one was intended: it must determine whether an execution path leaves any output holding its old value.

Consider a word selector with a two-bit opcode. Encodings \texttt{00} and \texttt{01} select operands, \texttt{10} selects their XOR, and \texttt{11} is reserved. The reserved code is still a physically possible binary input, so this interface specifies \texttt{valid=0} and a zero result for it. It makes the same choice for simulator-only meta-values without claiming that the manufactured circuit can detect a simulator's \texttt{x}. The choice of zero is an interface decision, not an instruction to the mapper that the output is a don't-care.

\section{Write the Reserved Case, Not Just the Happy Path}

The following SystemVerilog module is simulatable and normally synthesizable as combinational RTL \cite{ieee1800systemverilog}. It uses ordinary exact-match \texttt{case}, not wildcard matching.

\begin{verbatim}
module select_op (
    input  logic [1:0] op,
    input  logic [3:0] a, b,
    output logic [3:0] y,
    output logic       valid
);
    always_comb begin
        y     = 4'b0000;
        valid = 1'b1;
        case (op)
            2'b00: y = a;
            2'b01: y = b;
            2'b10: y = a ^ b;
            default: valid = 1'b0;
        endcase
    end
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

Defaults run first on every activation. The matching branch can override them, but there is never a path on which they are missing. Blocking assignments express these within-block data dependencies in order. The assignments from the three valid cases can be viewed as candidate inputs to a multiplexer. Kasprzyk, Koch, Golze, and Rock's \emph{An Improved Intermediate Representation for Datapath Generation}, section~6, similarly maps control-selected alternatives to multiplexers and discusses how changing their structure can affect depth. A source conditional describes selection; it need not map to a literal chain of physical switches.

Now remove only the initial assignment to \texttt{y}. The reserved and unknown-selector paths no longer assign it. Under simulation they preserve its previous value, and honoring this behavior for the binary reserved case requires level-sensitive storage. Tools may diagnose the latch as inconsistent with \verb|always_comb| and stop rather than emit it. Changing sensitivity syntax will not fix an assignment missing from a path. Compare the two meanings in \Cref{fig:visual-309}.

