\section{Test a Reserved Code After Two Different Histories}

\begin{verbatim}
drive a=3, b=10
for (legal, legal_y) in [(00,3), (01,10), (10,9)]:
    drive op=legal; settle and check (valid=1, y=legal_y)
    drive op=11; settle and check (valid=0, y=0)
    # A forgotten default preserves a different old y each time.
drive op=X0; settle and check (valid=0, y=0)
    # Exact match: X0 is not 00 or 10.
\end{verbatim}

The \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/synthesis/proc.html}{Yosys process conversion guide} distinguishes mux lowering from latch extraction. Inspect both simulation and diagnostics: a functional test can miss a latch if it never visits the unassigned branch, while a clean combinational netlist does not prove that the chosen reserved behavior matches the interface. Once the reserved output is explicitly zero, it is observable behavior rather than a synthesis don't-care; only an enclosing specification that declares it irrelevant could permit a tool to exploit that freedom. Avoid using wildcard \texttt{casex} to make a failing X test pass; it can treat a real modeling defect as a match and select a legal operation.
